# Text-to-Speech: Twinkle Twinkle

## Problem Title & Summary
Speak the "Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star" poem using a local text-to-speech
engine.

## Logic & Approach
The program imports `pyttsx3` and initializes its default speech engine. It
adds the poem text to the engine's queue with `engine.say(...)`, then calls
`engine.runAndWait()` to play the queued speech and wait for playback to
finish.

## Requirements
- Python 3
- The `pyttsx3` package
- A supported speech engine and voice installed on the operating system

Install the package with:
```text
python -m pip install pyttsx3
```

On Windows, `pyttsx3` normally uses SAPI5 voices. Other operating systems use
their supported speech engines.

## Time & Space Complexity
For poem text containing n characters, preparing and queueing the text takes
O(n) time and O(n) space. Speech playback time depends on the selected voice
and speaking rate.

## Example Input & Output
```text
Input: None

Output: The speech engine reads the poem aloud.
```

## Run
Run with Python 3:
```text
python text_to_speech.py
```

## Flow Diagram
```text
Start
  |
  v
Initialize speech engine
  |
  v
Queue poem text
  |
  v
Speak text and wait for playback
  |
  v
End
```

## Troubleshooting
- If Python reports `No module named 'pyttsx3'`, install the package using the
  command above with the same Python interpreter used to run the script.
- If initialization or playback fails, check that a supported speech engine
  and at least one usable voice are installed.
